chx:F1 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Chapter 17 of the 3rd edition, PDF and Word Documents, teaches PDF with the PyPDF package (extract text, post-process it with a language model, extract images, copy pages into a new file, rotate pages, insert blank pages, merge a watermark or overlay, encrypt and decrypt), Project 12 that combines select pages from many PDFs, and Word with the Python-Docx package (read paragraphs and runs, get the full text, style paragraphs and runs, apply run attributes, write paragraphs, headings, line and page breaks and pictures), and it ends with 12 practice questions and three practice programs, PDF Paranoia, Custom Invitations and PDF Password Breaker. The chapter page of 2026-10-12 is 100,057 bytes with 32 listings (19 of them interactive sessions of 91 statements), 10 captioned figures and 1 table, and its sha256, 00cb1643dca3afd3b75bfd9ce7ad594f3dd80f8606de1cffe40b8583846b4789, equals the chapterDigest of the book's own chapter ontology [P01] [P03] [P04]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P03, chx:P04 .
chx:F2 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's interactive sessions were replayed statement by statement, in order, on the files the book ships under Python 3.14.6 with pypdf 6.20.1 and python-docx 1.2.0: of 91 statements 89 printed what the page prints and 2 differ, both in the same place, the third run of the demo paragraph, which the page prints as ' and some ' and which is ' text and some ' in the shipped demo.docx. Under the versions Appendix A pins, pypdf 5.0.1 and python-docx 1.1.2, 88 match and 3 differ: the same two and writer.merge(2, 'Recursion_Chapter1.pdf', (0, 5)), which raises AttributeError: 'tuple' object has no attribute 'startswith'. The agreement is smaller than it looks: a statement that prints nothing cannot differ from the page, so the next finding concerns one that matches and still goes wrong [P01] [P02] [P05] [P06] [P19]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P05, chx:P06, chx:P19 .
chx:F3 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's merge() example, writer.merge(2, 'Recursion_Chapter1.pdf', (0, 5)), prints nothing and so matches the page under pypdf 6.20.1, but it does not do what the chapter says: the tuple lands in the third parameter, outline_item, so the library made a bookmark titled (0, 5) and inserted the whole 18-page file, and a writer of 5 pages ended with 23, identically on ten interpreters (3.11 to 3.14.6). With pages=(0, 5) the same call inserted 5 pages. append() accepts the tuple in its second place because the signature types its outline_item parameter as a title or a page range, and the signature of merge() types it as a title only. pypdf 5.0.1 raises AttributeError for the same line [P01] [P10] [P14] [P16]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P10, chx:P14, chx:P16 .
chx:F4 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Project 12 as printed does not run: the loop appends to pdf_files, a name never defined, and Python 3.14.6 answers NameError: name 'pdf_files' is not defined, suggesting pdf_filenames. The shipped combine_pdfs.py is not the finished program either: it stops at NameError: name 'PdfWriter' is not defined, because it writes PdfWriter without the pypdf prefix, and it also names pdf_files in its loop. With the name corrected the program combined three small files into combined.pdf of 5 pages (every page but the first of each), and a second run read combined.pdf as one of its own inputs and wrote 9 pages, which the program does not guard against. The test filename.endswith('.pdf') also misses a file named with capital letters [P01] [P02] [P35]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P35 .
chx:F5 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The book's two shipped scripts for text and images are the listings of the chapter, with Windows line endings: extractpdftext.py and extractpdfimages.py equal the printed text. Run on the shipped Recursion_Chapter1.pdf the text script wrote 32,981 characters in 688 lines and 5,363 words, with 11 warnings on the error stream that name fontTools, and the page texts are joined with no separator (page 1 ends with the words Al Sweigart and page 2 begins with 2 and Chapter 1, which run together as Al Sweigart2). The image script wrote 11 files of 2,099,648 bytes under the newest releases and 2,074,308 under the pinned ones, 7 images from page 1; the sample output the chapter prints says page 7 was skipped with not enough image data, and neither environment skipped it: both wrote 10_page7_Im0.tif. The zip does not hold encrypted.pdf, helloworld.docx or restyled.docx, which the chapter names as files the reader makes [P01] [P02] [P07] [P09]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P07, chx:P09 .
chx:F6 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Several statements about Word documents differ from the current package. The chapter prints a list of default style names (34 of them); a new document has 164 styles of four kinds (27 character, 1 list, 36 paragraph, 100 table), 'List Number ' with the trailing space the page prints raises KeyError, and MacroText is the id of a style named macro, which works only through a deprecated lookup by id that warns. A paragraph accepts only paragraph styles and a run only character styles: assigning 'Quote Char' to a paragraph or 'Quote' to a run raises ValueError. The paragraph list of a document does not include text in tables or headers, and text inside a hyperlink is in paragraph.text but in no run. The text script shipped as readDocx.py joins paragraphs with a blank line, so the output differs from the six lines the page prints [P01] [P02] [P20] [P21] [P22] [P24]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P20, chx:P21, chx:P22, chx:P24 .
chx:F7 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Table 17-1 lists twelve text attributes as attributes of a Run, and only three are: bold, italic and underline; strike, double_strike, all_caps, small_caps, shadow, outline, rtl, imprint and emboss exist on run.font. Assigning run.strike = True raises nothing and changes nothing (font.strike stayed None, no element in the XML), so the chapter's table sends a reader to a silent failure. The tri-state values are as the chapter says: None writes nothing, True writes <w:b/>, False writes <w:b w:val=0/>. The heading levels are given as 0 to 4 in one sentence and 1 to 9 in the next, and add_heading accepts 0 to 9 and raises ValueError outside it, which is also the answer to practice question 12 [P01] [P23] [P24]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P23, chx:P24 .
chx:F8 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Two optional dependencies and a default that the chapter does not mention matter in practice. page.images needs the Pillow package and raises ImportError without it. AES needs the cryptography package: without it AES-128 fails later, at write(), and AES-256 fails at encrypt(), both with DependencyError. encrypt() without an algorithm wrote RC4 with a 128-bit key (V 2, R 3), and algorithm='AES-256' wrote V 5, R 6 with qpdf reporting AESv3; the chapter shows only the AES-256 form. The chapter's text names neither Pillow nor cryptography, and the versions the book pins are given only in Appendix A, pypdf 5.0.1 and python-docx 1.1.2 [P01] [P05] [P09] [P11] [P13] [P41]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P05, chx:P09, chx:P11, chx:P13, chx:P41 .
chx:F9 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Text extraction is weaker than the chapter's tone suggests and the gap is documented: a PDF stores drawing commands, so the text comes out in stream order (worldHello for a page that shows Hello world), a black rectangle drawn over text leaves the text in the file, and a scanned page returned the empty string with one image. pypdf documents post-processing as outside its scope. On the sample chapter pdfminer.six returned 33,200 characters against pypdf's 32,981, put a form feed after every page and spaced out the chapter heading (W H A T), and of eleven damaged files none was read by pdfminer.six and refused by pypdf, so the fallback the chapter's listing offers was not needed in any of them [P07] [P08] [P27] [P40] [P44]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P07, chx:P08, chx:P27, chx:P40, chx:P44 .
chx:F10 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The first practice program, PDF Paranoia, takes its password from the command line, where any program that lists processes can read it (CWE-214), and deletes each original after a test decryption; written as the chapter asks, it encrypted the PDFs of a small folder tree with AES-256, removed the originals, skipped an already encrypted file, and decrypted with a wrong password without stopping. A filter on the lower-case extension .pdf misses C.PDF. The second practice program, Custom Invitations, needs styles that exist in the template; the study made them in code with styles.add_style, and a page break after every guest left no blank last page in LibreOffice 24.2, which counted 5 pages for 5 guests [P01] [P22] [P43] [P52]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P22, chx:P43, chx:P52 .
chx:F11 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The worst case of the PDF Password Breaker, run to the end on a file written by pypdf 6.20.1 with the book's dictionary.txt (349,900 words, both cases of each, so 699,800 attempts) and a password that is not in the list, found nothing and took 241.7 seconds for RC4, the default of encrypt(), and 1467.5 seconds for AES-256, the two runs started together on a machine with two cores. The page speaks of 88,000 attempts; the shipped file gives 699,800 (8.0 times the 44,000 words on the page), and a stronger algorithm costs about six times as much per guess, so the chapter's remark that a long random password defeats the program is shown by the timing, not only stated. Python 3.14.6." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P11, chx:P13 .
chx:F12 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The packages have moved on since the chapter's examples. The pypdf documentation tells that pyPdf (2005, Mathieu Fenniak) was forked as PyPDF2 at the end of 2011, that Martin Thoma took over PyPDF2 in April 2022 and that it was merged back into pypdf; PdfMerger, which older tutorials use, was removed in pypdf 5.0.0 (in 5.0.1 the name exists and raises DeprecationError, in 6.20.1 it is gone), and PdfFileReader and PdfFileWriter are not names of pypdf 6.20.1 either. Appendix A pins pypdf 5.0.1 and python-docx 1.1.2 while PyPI offers pypdf 6.20.1 and python-docx 1.2.0; an attempt to install Pillow 10.4.0 for the pinned environment found no wheel for Python 3.14 and the build failed for lack of libjpeg headers, so that environment used the newest Pillow. The package docx, which the chapter warns against, is at 0.2.4 from 6 February 2014 and its import fails under Python 3.14.6 with ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'exceptions' [P05] [P16] [P17] [P28] [P29] [P30]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P05, chx:P16, chx:P17, chx:P28, chx:P29, chx:P30 .
chx:F13 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "One program that exercises both packages was run under ten interpreters installed with uv: 3.11.17, 3.12.3, 3.13.16 and the seven releases 3.14.0 to 3.14.6, with the same pypdf, python-docx and pdfminer.six. Eleven results were equal in all ten: the page count (18), the images per page, the hash of the text from pypdf and from pdfminer.six, every pypdf operation (append (0, 5) gave 5 pages, (0, 5, 2) gave 3, [0, 5] gave 2, merge with pages=(0, 2) gave 4, rotation 90, encrypted file V 5 R 6), the python-docx operations, the silent ignoring of run.strike, the warning for the style id and the 23 pages of the chapter's merge line. The only difference was the build of the cryptography wheel on 3.13 (50.0.1 against 50.0.2). The chapter's PDF and Word behaviour does not depend on the 3.x release [P06] [P19] [P27] [P50]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P06, chx:P19, chx:P27, chx:P50 .
chx:F14 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "A PDF or Word file made by the chapter's listings opens elsewhere: LibreOffice 24.2 converted the seven Word files the study wrote (hello world, multiple paragraphs, headings, two pages, a picture, the restyled file and demo.docx) to PDF, counted 2 pages for the file with the page break and 1 for the others, and showed the text; qpdf --check accepted the PDF written by hand and by pypdf, and pdftotext without the password refused the encrypted file and read it with it. Microsoft Word itself, a PDF viewer and the language model clean-up of the section Post-Processing with AI were NOT EXECUTED, and the statement that the style names in the Styles pane of Word match the list the chapter prints is therefore checked only against the template of python-docx [P01] [P41] [P42] [P52]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P41, chx:P42, chx:P52 .
chx:F15 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Conclusion"@en ; sen0414:findingText "For an advanced course, chapter 17 is best taught as a limit on what can be automated: a PDF is a page description made for display and a .docx is a zip of XML parts, so a program can copy, reorder, rotate, stamp, encrypt, read the paragraphs and runs of and write a new document, and cannot recover structure that was never stored. The sections to correct are few and exact: pass pages= to merge(), rename pdf_files in Project 12 and guard against reading its own output, take heading levels as 0 to 9, set run attributes through run.font, use the style names the template has (no trailing space, no MacroText), name Pillow and cryptography, and keep the password of PDF Paranoia out of the command line. The sections to add are the file formats themselves, redaction that does not remove text, scanned pages, and the size of a brute-force attack. The 91 statements, the 12 corpus programs and the 70 examples of this study are the evidence [P01] [P06] [P19] [P40] [P43] [P44]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P06, chx:P19, chx:P40, chx:P43, chx:P44 .
chx:Scorecard a sen0414:QualityScorecard ; rdfs:label "Quality scorecard"@en ; sen0414:topicCoverage "85 primary-source concepts inventoried from the textbook ontology." ; sen0414:citationStrength "52 sources, all opened and verified at 2026-10-12T12:00:00; 33 cited by findings." ; sen0414:methodologyCompliance "RDODI procedure v1.6.0 Stage 1." ; sen0414:reproducibility "Every source is a stable public URL; every claim names its sources." .
